  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWe're beginning to see the organization of the strong system\nthat will bring gusty, strong winds and rainfall through the\narea today and into Friday. Accompanying this strong system is\nthe presence of a strong low level jet, with modeled values of \nover 60 knots over the NE PA region, which is higher \ncomparatively to yesterday's guidance. Similarly, confidence has\nincreased that wind gust values will be higher than yesterday's\nforecast in the NE PA region, resulting in the upgrade to a \nHigh Wind Warning for Luzerne-Lackawanna-Wyoming counties. When \ntranslating to the ground, up to 60 mph wind gusts are possible \nin these counties, and up to 50 to 55 mph in surrounding NE PA \ncounties. As this jet lifts north and weakens a little, gusts \nwill be a little lower, but up to 40 to 50 mph gusts are \npossible across much of Central NY. A few counties have been \nincluded in the existing Wind Advisories, expanding westward \ninto Onondaga-Tompkins-Southern Cayuga counties. The current \nestimated timing for strongest winds will be between this \nafternoon and late evening, with winds tapering off into the \nearly morning hours of Friday.\n\nFor the rain forecast, overall amounts have decreased a bit,\nmainly due to a dry slot that's expected to push into our area\nearly morning Friday. Though, moderate and occasionally heavy \nrainfall is possible later this morning for southern areas, \nspreading northward through the day. Rain may not begin in \nnorthern Central NY until the late afternoon/early evening \nhours. Scattered rain showers is expected to continue into \nFriday evening and potentially into Saturday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2....\n\nAlthough much weaker than the storm system impacting our area \ntoday, another weather system is expected Sunday night into \nMonday. The low looks to track just west or right over the area \nearly Monday morning with a period of rain likely. Temperatures \nwill be mild, so it is expected to be all rain Sunday night. \nSome cooler air will gradually filter into the area on Monday \nbehind the cold front, so some of the lingering rain showers \ncould perhaps mix with some wet snow flakes later Monday into \nMonday night before ending. Temperatures will still be mild in \nthe mid-40s to around 50 on Monday though. It cools off into the\nupper 20s to lower 30s by Monday night.\n\nHigh pressure builds in from the west on Tuesday into Wednesday next \nweek, so other than some lingering lake effect sprinkles or \nflurries on Tuesday it should dry out under partly sunny skies \nwith seasonable temperatures.",
